// Compiler options for the specs, used by ts-jest. Identical to the build's, except that the specs
// get jest's globals. Keeping them out of tsconfig.json is what makes a test-only identifier left in
// shipped source a compile error rather than something that builds cleanly.
//
// The file list is the specs plus the ambient declarations, and both parts are load-bearing.
//
// ts-jest builds its program from this list rather than only reading `compilerOptions`, so dropping
// `src/typings.d.ts` takes the iframe-phone shim out of scope and every suite fails to compile with
// TS2575 at the `new IframePhoneRpcEndpoint(...)` call. Ambient declarations are reached by being
// included, never by being imported.
//
// `exclude` is reset because it is inherited, and the base excludes exactly the spec files this
// project is for. Left inherited, this resolves to the library sources and no specs at all, which
// type-checks shipped code with jest's globals in scope — the hole the split exists to close — and
// emits it into the `build/` directory rollup reads.
//
// The library files the specs import are still pulled in, and `types` is program-wide, so they see
// jest's globals here too. That is unavoidable, and is why the guard lives in the base config that
// the build uses rather than in this one.
{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["jest"]
  },
  "include": ["./src/**/*.spec.ts", "./src/**/*.test.ts", "./src/**/*.d.ts"],
  "exclude": []
}
